.TH QIBOCAMP 1 "October 23, 2026" "qibocamp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
qibocamp \- serve calibration reports and dispatch interactive notebooks
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B qibocamp
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.I COMMAND
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Qibocamp provides the qibocamp command for report servers, local
configuration management, notebook sessions, and agent configurations.
Every configuration file is a JSON object carrying its format version,
{"version": 1, ...}; a file without a version, or with another one, is not
read and never overwritten. Use
.B qibocamp COMMAND \-\-help
for the available command options.
.SH NOTEBOOK
.B qibocamp notebook connect
.RI [ CONNECTION ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
Start JupyterLab (default) or Marimo and open its authenticated URL in the
local browser. Keep the command running for the lifetime of the session.
Ctrl+C stops the notebook, releases its SLURM allocation,
and closes the tunnels.
.PP
The connection layers compose:
local machine, optional SSH access node, optional SLURM computing node.
Omitting SSH submits from the local machine. Omitting SLURM runs on the access
node. Omitting both starts a local notebook.
.PP
Each worker immediately logs its node identity to standard error:
actual hostname, fully qualified domain name, user, OS release, architecture,
Python executable and version, process ID, and working directory on arrival.
SLURM sessions distinguish access and compute nodes and include available
allocation details: cluster, job and step IDs, partition, node lists,
CPU and memory allocation, and GPU IDs.
Without SLURM, one node serves both access and compute roles.
.PP
Node details appear in labeled Rich panels, with colored startup stages
and a ready banner. All formatting happens locally; remote workers forward
structured diagnostics without requiring Rich or a terminal on SSH or SLURM
nodes. Redirected output has no automatic ANSI colors.
Diagnostics remain on standard error and the authenticated URL remains
on its own line on standard output.
.SS Connect options
.TP
.BI \-\-ssh " TEXT"
SSH options followed by a host or user@host destination.
Quote the entire value when including options, for example
.BR "\-\-ssh '\-p 2222 \-i ~/.ssh/lab user@login'" .
Host aliases, agent authentication, and jump hosts from SSH configuration work.
The destination must be the last argument; do not include a remote command.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fIPARTITION\fR
SLURM partition passed to srun on the access node.
.TP
.BI \-\-slurm " TEXT"
Additional srun options as a quoted argument string, for example
.BR "\-\-slurm '\-\-time=02:00:00 \-\-account=lab \-\-gres=gpu:1'" .
Specifying this option alone also enables SLURM dispatch.
Resource allocation must select one task on one computing node.
Only common scheduling/resource options are accepted; command, IO,
and working-directory overrides are rejected. Use separated short options
(for example, \-c 2 rather than \-c2).
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-workdir " " \fIDIRECTORY\fR
Change directory on the access node before starting the session or submitting
to SLURM. With SLURM, this directory must also be accessible on the computing
node. Relative kernel paths are resolved in the notebook working directory.
.TP
.B \-\-marimo
Run Marimo instead of JupyterLab.
.TP
.B \-\-jupyter
Use JupyterLab, including when overriding a named Marimo connection.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-interactive
Print the authenticated URL without opening a browser. The command still
stays attached to supervise the notebook and tunnels.
.TP
.B \-\-interactive
Open the browser, including when overriding a named non-interactive connection.
.TP
.BI \-\-venv " NAME_OR_PATH"
Kernel environment on the computing node; default: for a named connection,
its name made safe for a folder (letters, digits, dots, underscores and
hyphens, any other run of characters becoming a hyphen), so distinct
connections have distinct environments; otherwise
.BR qibocal .
Names resolve under
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/qibocamp/envs/
(fallback:
.BR ~/.cache/qibocamp/envs/ ).
Absolute paths and explicit relative paths (./env or ../env) are used directly.
Existing environments are never overwritten. Jupyter leaves target environments
unchanged; with \-\-marimo, Marimo is installed directly into the target environment.
Any missing kernel environment, whether specified by name or path, is created with
the latest public Qibocal release using pip install qibocal.
You can replace it with a development version or manage its dependencies
afterward.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
Maximum startup time, including environment preparation, queue wait, and
tunnel setup. Default: 300. Increase this for long SLURM queue waits.
.TP
.BI \-\-subnet " CIDR"
Restrict SLURM computing-node IP selection to an IPv4 or IPv6 subnet,
for example 192.168.0.0/24. Among matching addresses, choose the IP with the
longest network prefix in common with an access-node IP.
Startup fails if no usable computing-node IP matches.
Local and unscheduled SSH sessions still bind only to loopback.
.TP
.BI \-\-reports " DIR"
Where the session keeps its reports, relative to the working directory:
created when the session starts, and exported to the notebook and its
kernels as QIBOCAMP_REPORTS_DIR. The working directory is unchanged.
.TP
.BI \-\-remote " NAME"
A remote execution connection of remotes.json, for qibocal-widgets: exported
to the notebook as QIBOCAL_WIDGETS_CONNECTION, and written to remotes.json
where the notebook runs. Unless it names one, the connection has an environment
of its own, named after it as a notebook connection is, under
.BR _remote/ .
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild\-on\-compute
Make
.B qibocamp notebook rebuild
regenerate the environment of a SLURM connection on a computing node, through
srun.
.TP
.B \-\-rebuild\-on\-access
Regenerate it on the access node (default).
.TP
.BI \-\-requirements " TEXT|@FILE"
What the kernel environment must hold, in requirements.txt format: the text
itself, or @FILE to read it from a local file. Stored with a named connection
in notebooks.json. Installed with pip when the environment is created; at
every later session, if the requirements differ from those last installed in
that environment, they are installed on top of it. The environment is never
rebuilt and nothing is uninstalled; a failed installation stops the session
and is tried again at the next one.
.SS Requirements and environments
Local execution requires Python 3.10+ with venv support. SSH execution requires
OpenSSH locally and python3 (3.10+) with venv support on the access
and computing nodes. SLURM execution requires srun on the access node
and network access from the access node to the allocated computing node.
Both nodes need hostname \-I to report their interface addresses.
The launcher selects a computing-node IP by the longest common network prefix
with an access-node address; hostname resolution and SSH access to the computing
node are not required.
Address discovery and the notebook run in the same single SLURM allocation.
Local and unscheduled SSH notebook servers bind only to loopback.
SLURM notebook servers bind to the selected computing-node interface, not all
interfaces, and are protected by a generated authentication token.
The access worker relays its loopback port to that interface; SSH sessions
tunnel access-node loopback to the local machine. The access-to-compute network
hop is not encrypted, so use SLURM notebooks only on a trusted computing network.
Every session uses a generated authentication token.
.PP
Jupyter server dependencies are installed on first use into the separate managed
jupyter environment under $XDG_CACHE_HOME/qibocamp/envs/
(fallback: ~/.cache/qibocamp/envs/), not the kernel environment.
Marimo is installed directly into the target environment and launched with its
Python; no separate Marimo environment is used. Installing notebook dependencies or
Qibocal into a new kernel environment requires package-index access
on the computing node and either ensurepip or pip (22.3+) in the worker's Python.
Environments are created with uv venv \-\-seed and filled with uv pip install,
using the uv on the PATH or one installed once under
$XDG_CACHE_HOME/qibocamp/tools/uv; without either, or when QIBOCAMP_NO_UV is set,
with venv and pip as described here.
All newly created environments include pip. If ensurepip is unavailable,
the worker uses the external installer to install pip and any requested
dependencies into the new environment; system packages
are not changed. This requires package-index access even for otherwise empty
kernel environments. Existing Jupyter server environments are reused as-is.
If first-time installation fails, repair that server environment explicitly
using the command reported by the launcher; it is never implicitly overwritten.
Jupyter exposes all managed kernel environments and environments previously selected
for Jupyter with \-\-venv on the computing node. A named connection defaults
to an environment of its own, named after it (see \-\-venv). The environments
of remote execution connections, kept under
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/qibocamp/envs/_remote/ ,
are not offered as kernels.
The selected environment is the default kernel. The managed qibocal environment is
named Qibocal; other managed kernels are named Qibocal (NAME).
Path-based kernels also include their path in the label, with the home directory
abbreviated as ~.
Registrations persist under $XDG_CACHE_HOME/qibocamp/kernels/
(fallback: ~/.cache/qibocamp/kernels/).
Unavailable environments and the Jupyter server environment are not listed.
Each kernel uses its environment's Python and packages, with server-provided
ipykernel dependencies as a fallback.
This keeps an existing Jupyter calibration environment unchanged; server dependencies
may still appear in the notebook's import path.
.SS Named connections
Create a JSON object with the format version and, under connections, an
object whose keys are connection names and values are options:
.PP
.nf
{
  "version": 1,
  "connections": {
    "mychip": {
      "ssh": "myuser@login",
      "queue": "mychip",
      "workdir": "/shared/calibration",
      "venv": "qibocal",
      "slurm": "--time=02:00:00",
      "marimo": false,
      "no_interactive": false,
      "timeout": 900
    }
  }
}
.fi
.PP
Save it to
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/qibocamp/notebooks.json ,
with
.BR ~/.config/qibocamp/notebooks.json
as the fallback. QIBOCAMP_CONFIG_DIR overrides its containing directory.
Invoke
.BR "qibocamp notebook connect mychip" .
Explicit command-line options override the connection's values.
Unknown options, invalid types, missing names, and malformed files are errors.
A file without a version, or with another one, is not read: it holds no
connections, and it is never overwritten.
The connect command does not create or modify the connection file.
.SS Adding connections
.B qibocamp notebook add
.RI [ CONNECTION ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
With no options, prompt first for the connection name if not supplied, then show a
multiple-selection menu of options to change from their defaults, then ask for
each selected value. Unselected options retain their defaults.
.PP
To register a connection without the setup menu, provide its name and options
together. The options are identical to those of
.BR "qibocamp notebook connect" ;
see Connect options above or
.BR "qibocamp notebook connect \-\-help" .
If options are provided without a name, prompt only for the name.
A name alone opens the setup menu for that connection.
Explicitly supplied options are saved in their JSON configuration representation.
Other connections are preserved; an existing name is an error.
Adding a connection does not start a notebook session.
.SS Listing connections
.B qibocamp notebook list
.RB [ \-\-raw ]
.PP
Display registered names and their configured options in a Rich table.
An empty registry is reported without creating a file.
With \-\-raw, print the connection file verbatim, without formatting or JSON
validation; a missing file is an error.
.SS Updating connections
.B qibocamp notebook update
.RI [ CONNECTION ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
Update an existing connection using the same options as connect.
Explicitly supplied settings replace their stored values; unspecified settings
and other connections are preserved. With no options, show the option-selection
menu using the connection's current values. Prompt for the name if omitted.
Missing connections and invalid resulting options are errors.
Updating a connection does not start or stop a notebook session.
.SS Removing connections
.B qibocamp notebook remove
.I CONNECTION
.PP
Remove the named connection immediately, without confirmation.
The name must exist. Other connections, environments, and running sessions
are unchanged. Removing the last connection leaves an empty connections object.
.SS Rebuilding an environment
.B qibocamp notebook rebuild
.RI [ CONNECTION ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
Remove the kernel environment of a connection entirely and create it again
with qibocal and the \-\-requirements alone, in a single install. A SLURM
connection is regenerated on the access node, which shares the home the
environment lives in, unless \-\-rebuild\-on\-compute asks for a computing
node, through srun. No notebook is started. Only a directory holding
pyvenv.cfg is removed; the notebook server environment is never touched.
.SS Looking at a queue
.B qibocamp notebook queue
.RI [ CONNECTION ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
What a SLURM queue is doing, asked once: squeue, sinfo and whoami run in a
single round trip, over SSH when a destination is given, and the answer is
printed as a table. The host and the partition come from a saved connection,
or from the options. Ask sparingly: every call costs the controller something.
Options:
.BI \-\-ssh " TEXT" ,
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fIPARTITION\fR
(without it, every partition),
.BR \-u ", " \-\-user " " \fIUSER\fR
(only the jobs of this user),
.BR \-t ", " \-\-state " " \fISTATE\fR
(only jobs in this state, repeatable: PENDING, RUNNING, ...),
.B \-\-json
(the answer as JSON),
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
(seconds to wait for the controller; default: 30).
.PP
.B qibocamp notebook queues
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.PP
What a machine's SLURM queues are doing, asked once: the jobs of each
partition given, and a line for every partition of the machine \(em its nodes
and jobs by state, and how many are yours. One squeue for the whole machine,
filtered on it, so only the jobs asked for travel back. Options:
.BI \-\-ssh " TEXT"
(the machine; without it, this one),
.BR \-q ", " \-\-queue " " \fIPARTITION\fR
(a partition whose jobs are wanted, repeatable),
.B \-\-json
and
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS" .
.SS Examples
.nf
qibocamp notebook connect
qibocamp notebook connect --venv ./calibration-env --marimo
qibocamp notebook connect --ssh myuser@login --workdir /shared/runs
qibocamp notebook connect --queue mychip --no-interactive
qibocamp notebook connect --ssh myuser@login -q mychip --timeout 900
qibocamp notebook connect mychip --jupyter -n
qibocamp notebook add
qibocamp notebook add mychip --ssh myuser@login -q mychip
qibocamp notebook list
qibocamp notebook list --raw
qibocamp notebook update mychip --subnet 192.168.0.0/24
qibocamp notebook update mychip --requirements @requirements.txt
qibocamp notebook rebuild mychip
qibocamp notebook remove mychip
qibocamp notebook queue mychip --user myuser
.fi
.SH REPORT COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "qibocamp report server" " [DIRECTORY]"
Serve reports, here or on another machine. The server is headless: it
serves the API the dashboard reads, and the dashboard, deployed at
.B https://qibo.science/qibocamp
and embedded in the desktop application, is given the server's address.
Without a directory, the shared reports directory is served:
.BR $QIBOCAMP_DATA_DIR/reports ,
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/qibocamp/reports
or
.BR ~/.local/share/qibocamp/reports ,
created when missing. A named directory has to exist.
Options: \-\-host (localhost), \-\-port (8000), \-\-auth/\-\-no-auth
(default: no auth; with \-\-auth the initial administrator invitation
is printed),
.BI \-\-name " TEXT"
(the name the server is registered under),
\-\-register/\-\-no-register (whether it goes into servers.json at all;
default: register), and the remote options below.
.RS
.PP
With
.BI \-\-ssh " TEXT"
the directory is read on that host and the server is tunnelled back here:
the destination comes last, as for
.BR "qibocamp notebook connect" ,
the remote server binds to its own loopback on a free port, and the local end
of the tunnel is the address registered in servers.json. \-\-host and \-\-port
then do not apply. The remote host needs an OpenSSH server and python3 (3.10+).
.PP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-workdir " " \fIDIRECTORY\fR
sets the remote working directory (default: a fresh temporary directory).
.BI \-\-venv " NAME_OR_PATH"
selects the remote environment (default: qibocamp), resolved under
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME/qibocamp/envs/
(fallback:
.BR ~/.cache/qibocamp/envs/ )
as the notebook sessions resolve it; a missing environment is created, and an
existing one without qibocamp has it installed.
.BI \-\-install " TEXT"
names the packages installed in that case (default: this qibocamp,
from GitHub: the commit it was installed from, or main), and
.BI \-\-timeout " SECONDS"
bounds the startup, environment preparation included (default: 300).
These options are refused without \-\-ssh.
.RE
.TP
.B qibocamp report server upgrade
Bring a remote server's qibocamp up to date, without starting it: the
\-\-install packages are put back by force in the \-\-venv environment on the
\-\-ssh host, the packages themselves first, then their dependencies. That
environment otherwise keeps what it holds, since it is only filled when it has
no qibocamp. Nothing is served; start the server as usual afterwards.
Options:
.BI \-\-ssh " TEXT"
(required), \-w/\-\-workdir, \-\-venv (qibocamp),
\-\-install (qibocamp), \-\-timeout (300), as for
.BR "qibocamp report server" .
.TP
.BR "qibocamp report upload" " PATH SERVER"
Upload a report to a server, as the dashboard's Upload does: PATH is a report
folder, holding a meta.json, sent under its own name, or the zip of one, as a
download gives it. A report of the same name there is never overwritten.
.BI \-\-token " TOKEN"
signs in with a token (also QIBOCAMP_TOKEN);
.BI \-u ", " \-\-username " NAME"
signs in as a user, the password asked or taken from QIBOCAMP_PASSWORD;
.B \-\-json
answers with the server's reply.
.TP
.BR "qibocamp report targets" " [DIRECTORY]"
Gather each report's targets from its protocols: every protocol's action.yml
under the directory (default: the shared reports directory) names the qubits,
or pairs, it was run on, and their union goes into the report's meta.json as
targets-inferred, next to the session's own targets. The dashboard shows the
inferred targets first.
.TP
.BR "qibocamp report dev" " [DIRECTORY]"
Run backend and Vite development servers. The directory defaults to the
shared reports directory, as for
.BR "qibocamp report server" .
Options: \-\-host (localhost),
\-\-port (8000), \-\-frontend-port (5173), \-\-reload/\-\-no-reload
(default: reload), \-\-auth/\-\-no-auth, \-\-name, \-\-register/\-\-no-register.
Requires the frontend sources and Node.js.
.TP
.B qibocamp report admin list
List administrators. Options: \-\-all (include all users), \-\-json,
and \-\-ssh, \-\-venv (qibocamp) for a server on another machine:
its administrators are listed there.
.TP
.BR "qibocamp report admin invite" " [USERNAME]"
Generate an administrator invitation. The link opens the dashboard,
.BR $QIBOCAMP_DASHBOARD_URL/#/invite?token=...&server=... .
Options: \-u/\-\-username,
\-\-server-url, \-\-host, \-\-port, \-e/\-\-expires-in-hours (168, 0 for
never), \-\-interactive/\-\-no-interactive, \-\-json, and \-\-ssh,
\-\-venv (qibocamp) for a server on another machine: an invitation is
valid only in the users of the server's own machine, so it is made there.
.BR "qibocamp report invite"
is a shortcut, without \-\-ssh and \-\-venv.
.SH AGENTS COMMANDS
.TP
.BR "qibocamp agents push" " SSH"
Write qibocal-agent configurations into agents.json on the SSH destination.
.BI \-\-agents " JSON"
gives the entries to write, as an object of agents.NAME entries, merged into
the remote agents;
.BI \-\-remove " NAME" ,
repeatable, drops one there. Nothing else in the remote file changes, and
nothing but the configuration travels, never environments or keys.
.TP
.B qibocamp agents install
Install qibocal-agent where the desktop application keeps it,
.IR ~/.cache/qibocal/agents/tools ,
and print the path of its executable: the command an assistant registers. The
environment is created when missing, with Python 3.12 or 3.13, which uv
provides when needed;
.BI \-\-ref " REF" ,
a branch, tag or commit of qibocal-mcp-servers (default: main), replaces what
was installed.
.SH CONFIG COMMANDS
.TP
.B qibocamp config path
Print the absolute report configuration directory without creating it.
.TP
.B qibocamp config clean
Ask before removing servers.json and authentication data.
\-f/\-\-force skips confirmation. Does not remove notebooks.json, notebook
environments, reports, unrelated files, or directories.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_NO_UV
When set, environments are created and filled with venv and pip, and uv is
neither used nor installed.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Configuration root for notebook connections.
.TP
.B XDG_CACHE_HOME
Cache root for computing-node kernel and notebook server environments.
Each node uses its own value; the local value is not forwarded over SSH.
.TP
.B XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
Location for temporary notebook kernel specifications on the computing node.
When unset, empty, or pointing to a missing directory, use the system temporary
directory. A missing directory is reported in the startup diagnostics.
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_CONFIG_DIR
Override the local report and notebook connection configuration directory.
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_AUTH_FILE
Override the report authentication database; also removed by
.BR "qibocamp config clean" .
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_DIR
Report directory used by the report server.
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_DATA_DIR
Override the data directory, whose reports/ subfolder is served when no
directory is named. Default:
.B $XDG_DATA_HOME/qibocamp/
(fallback:
.BR ~/.local/share/qibocamp/ ).
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Data root for the shared reports directory.
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_DASHBOARD_URL
The dashboard that invitation links open, told the server's address.
Default:
.BR https://qibo.science/qibocamp .
.TP
.B QIBOCAMP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS
Further origins whose pages may call the server from a browser,
comma-separated. The dashboard of QIBOCAMP_DASHBOARD_URL, the desktop launcher
and pages of this machine (localhost) always may; any other page is refused,
and a change it asks for, an upload included, is answered 403.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on normal completion or an orderly notebook Ctrl+C shutdown.
Nonzero on invalid configuration, startup failure, timeout, or session failure.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh (1),
.BR srun (1),
.BR python3 (1)
